Z||000002||88 Fiero GT Turbo||09-23-2000||02:44 PM||||it does not matter why I am changing them but i was told that you should have your chip burned to the new size injector.  Is it true or what???????????????<P>Later,<BR>Jack<P>88 Fiero GT Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000003||fiero56||09-23-2000||06:45 PM||||It VERY MUCH depends on why you are changing them. Are you changing them to fix a fuel/air mixture problem? If so, maybe not. If not, why are you changing them?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 09-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||09-24-2000||12:22 PM||||Really does not matter. Yhe only thing I would recommend is get an adjustrable fuel pressure regulator and increase your fuel pressure to around 45. As far as the chip, don't waste your money. I install 3.4 all day long and use a 305 injector with a n adjustable fuel pressure regulator. Never have had to replace chip. That is a myth.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||09-25-2000||08:12 AM||||i had a chip burned just so my car would run on the bigger injectors. (it wouldn't idle or any thing.. 3 hours later after some dyno tweaking we got the parameters right and laid them on an e-prom.. and POOF... faster car.. it's much easier to get an injector controller... or pulse monitor and make sure your really using what you have... or to tune what you already have... <A HREF="http://www.splitsec.com" TARGET=_blank>www.splitsec.com</A>   but if you have a forced induction car you'll need a rising rate boost sensative fuel pressure controller.. such as the Vortch fmu or the SFMU. along with an MSD 6BTM to aid in keeping detonation away.<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
